    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Sport pressures and dietary restraint were highly stable over the course of a competitive season. The pressures gymnasts and swimmers/divers experienced regarding body weight, size, and appearance from their coaches and teammates predicted decreases in their body satisfaction over a 5-month period; this change occurred even though the athletes' body mass index did not change significantly over that time."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "First, we included only NCAA Division I female gymnasts and swimmers/divers, who also happened to primarily be Caucasian. Future research should examine whether our findings are supported in other groups of female athletes (e.g., different sports, different competitive levels, more diverse racial/ethnic groups); male athletes also should be studied and such research should include measures of drive for muscularity. Second, we examined the variables over only a 5-month period, which represented one competitive collegiate season for these athletes. Although an important first step, other longitudinal studies may want to examine eating disorders and other potential risk factors over a longer period of time (e.g., collegiate career), or a different period of time (e.g., from the end of the competitive season to the start of the next one). Whenever possible, researchers may want to obtain measures at more than two points to determine more specifically how the variables under study fluctuate over time. Third, we examined the relationships among only three LVs, specifically sport pressures, body dissatisfaction, and dietary restraint. Although these variables have been related to disordered eating (e.g., Anderson et al., 2011), additional research is needed to examine other LVs, such as general societal pressures, internalization, and negative affect to ascertain which variables are risk factors in the development of eating disorder symptoms and which ones mediate the effects of the others (Stice, 2002). Fourth, the validity of self-report measures of dietary restraint as predictors of actual reductions in food intake has been challenged (see Stice, Fisher, & Lowe, 2004; Stice, Presnell, Lowe, & Burton, 2006; van Strien, Engels, van Staveren, & Herman, 2006)."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Programs that target general societal, as well as sport-specific, ideals, pressures, and messages about body, weight, and appearance, and what it means to be a female athlete, will be a necessary next step to prevent the development of disordered eating attitudes and behaviors, and should be the focus of researchers."
    }
